WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.473
The takeaway is simple and actionable.

00:00:02.553 --> 00:00:08.520
Stochastic gradient noise is completely harmless
for the one-step training loss you watch,

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.745
but once your model sits inside a feedback loop,

00:00:11.825 --> 00:00:14.702
that same noise gets amplified into chaotic,

00:00:14.782 --> 00:00:17.456
catastrophic failures over long horizons.

00:00:17.536 --> 00:00:20.614
They call this gradient variance amplification.

00:00:20.694 --> 00:00:23.638
The good news is that the fix is almost free.

00:00:23.718 --> 00:00:28.476
Keep an exponential moving average of your weights,
and you get a cheap,

00:00:28.556 --> 00:00:30.424
broadly effective stabilizer.

00:00:30.504 --> 00:00:34.724
Whether you are training a robot controller or a
language model,

00:00:34.804 --> 00:00:39.091
iterate averaging deserves to be a standard part of
the pipeline,

00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:43.190
right next to your optimizer and your learning rate
schedule.
